\description{
Computes Lin's (1989) concordance correlation coefficient (CCC) for
a pair of vectors of paired observations, together with a confidence
interval built on Lin's \emph{z}-transformed standard error (Lin,
1989; see also the note in Lin, 2000). The CCC measures agreement (not
merely correlation) between two methods of measurement: a CCC of 1
means perfect agreement (\eqn{y_i = x_i} for all \eqn{i}), while
Pearson's \eqn{r} would still be 1 for any straight-line relationship,
even one with non-unit slope.
}
\details{
\strong{Definition.} Lin (1989) defined the CCC as
\deqn{\rho_c \;=\;
  \frac{2 \rho\, \sigma_x \sigma_y}
       {\sigma_x^2 + \sigma_y^2 + (\mu_x - \mu_y)^2},}
where \eqn{\rho = \mathrm{Cor}(X, Y)} is the Pearson correlation and
the denominator is inflated by the squared mean difference and by any
inequality of the two variances, so disagreement in location or scale
pulls \eqn{\rho_c} below \eqn{\rho}. \eqn{\rho_c} factors as \eqn{\rho_c = \rho \cdot C_b},
where \eqn{C_b \in [0, 1]} is the "bias correction factor" that
captures location and scale agreement, and \eqn{C_b = 1} iff
\eqn{\mu_x = \mu_y} and \eqn{\sigma_x = \sigma_y}.

\strong{Confidence interval.} The Fisher-style \emph{z}-transform of
the CCC, \eqn{z = \frac{1}{2} \log\{(1 + \rho_c)/(1 - \rho_c)\}}, has
approximate variance (Lin, 1989, as corrected in Lin, 2000)
\deqn{\mathrm{Var}(z) \;\approx\;
  \frac{1}{n - 2}
  \left[ \frac{(1 - \rho^2) \rho_c^2}{(1 - \rho_c^2) \rho^2}
       + \frac{2 \rho_c^3 (1 - \rho_c) u^2}{\rho (1 - \rho_c^2)^2}
       - \frac{\rho_c^4 u^4}{2 \rho^2 (1 - \rho_c^2)^2}\right],}
where \eqn{u = (\mu_x - \mu_y) / \sqrt{\sigma_x \sigma_y}}. The CI is
built on the \emph{z}-scale and back-transformed via \eqn{\tanh}.

This variance is derived under bivariate normality, and the interval
inherits that assumption. Under normality its coverage is modestly
below the nominal rate in small samples (roughly 0.92 to 0.94 at
\eqn{n} of 10 to 20 for a nominal 0.95) and approaches the nominal
rate as \eqn{n} grows (about 0.94 at \eqn{n = 50} for a moderate
CCC). With clearly skewed data the situation is
worse and more data do not repair it: with heavy-tailed or log-normal
style measurements the interval can cover far below the nominal rate
at any sample size (Carrasco, Jover, King, & Chinchilli, 2007). With
such data, transform toward symmetry before computing the CCC, or use
a bootstrap interval on \eqn{\hat\rho_c}.
}
